tation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **code-review: quantified health score (0\u2013100) with severity-weighted deductions on review verdict** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md \u2014 pattern: Health Score algorithm with severity-weighted deductions\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-10\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-17)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n**Priority**: P1\\n**Score**: 6/10 \u2014 Additive improvement that does not break existing verdict routing. High signal value: replaces ambiguous NEEDS-WORK states with a quantified score enabling trend analysis. Soft dependency on #2250 for canonical severity table.\\n**Rationale**: P1 is appropriate \u2014 the item unblocks trend analysis in `forensic-review` remediation cycles and is a prerequisite for fail-on-threshold CI gate patterns. Can proceed independently of #2250.\\n**Effort**: Small \u2014 three insertion points in one agent markdown file (`code-reviewer.md`). No new files, no schema changes, no new MCP tools. Algorithm fully specified. Estimated: 1 session (2\u20134 hours).\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: none \u2014 Health Score is additive, ternary verdict routing unchanged\\n- Enables: trend analysis in `forensic-review` remediation cycles; prerequisite pattern for fail-on-threshold checks (`fail-below: 70`) in CI-adjacent workflows\\n- Bottleneck: two consecutive `NEEDS-WORK` reviews are currently indistinguishable \u2014 orchestrators cannot tell whether remediation improved, regressed, or stalled\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Orchestrators can detect improving vs regressing code across remediation cycles without reading the full finding list\\n- A single number (`Health Score: 35/100`) communicates severity distribution faster than counting finding lists\\n- Establishes the scoring foundation that CI-adjacent fail-on-threshold checks will consume\\n- Aligns the dh `code-reviewer` output with the brooks-lint scoring model\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nThe `code-reviewer` agent, after computing a verdict in Step 7, calculates a Health Score: starting from 100, subtract 15 per Critical finding, 5 per Warning finding, 1 per Suggestion finding, floor 0.\\n\\nThe score appears in:\\n1. The `codebase-analysis` artifact \u2014 a `## Health Score` section with the numeric score and per-category deduction table\\n2. The STATUS output block \u2014 a `Health Score: NN/100` line inside ARTIFACTS\\n\\nThe ternary verdict (`PASS` / `NEEDS-WORK` / `FAIL`) remains the primary routing signal. The Health Score is additive and does not replace the verdict.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nAfter implementation:\\n\\n1. `agents/code-reviewer.md` Step 7 contains a Health Score calculation rule alongside the verdict table\\n2. `agents/code-reviewer.md` Output Format Summary section contains a `Health Score:` field\\n3. `agents/code-reviewer.md` STATUS block contains a `Health Score: NN/100` line\\n4. The `codebase-analysis` artifact contains a `## Health Score` section with score and deduction breakdown\\n5. Running the agent on 2 Critical + 1 Warning findings produces `Health Score: 65/100`\\n6. No existing `PASS`/`NEEDS-WORK`/`FAIL` routing logic in `forensic-review/SKILL.md` is changed\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `grep -c 'Health Score' plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` returns 3 or more (Step 7, Output Format, STATUS block)\\n2. Running `@dh:code-reviewer` on a sample with exactly 2 Critical + 1 Warning findings produces `Health Score: 65/100` in STATUS and artifact\\n3. Running the agent on a clean sample (no findings) produces `Health Score: 100/100`\\n4. Running the agent on a sample with enough findings to exceed 100 deduction points produces `Health Score: 0/100` (floor enforced)\\n5. The `codebase-analysis` artifact for AC2 contains a `## Health Score` section with per-finding deduction table\\n6. `forensic-review/SKILL.md` Step 3 verdict routing (`PASS`/`NEEDS-WORK`/`FAIL`) is unchanged\\n7. The Output Format section in `code-reviewer.md` is self-consistent: markdown template matches what AC2 produces\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Primary source | `research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md` lines 136\u2013148 \u2014 Health Score algorithm with severity deductions |\\n| Agent to modify | `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` \u2014 Step 7 (line 151), Output Format (line 182), STATUS block (line 237) |\\n| Orchestration consumer | `plugins/development-harness/skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md` \u2014 Step 3 verdict consumption |\\n| Related item | #2250 \u2014 `code-review-shared` skill (will own canonical severity tier table) |\\n| Related item | #2248 \u2014 Iron Law (defines Critical/Warning/Suggestion classification) |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 severity algorithm fully specified; can be implemented independently of #2250\\n- Soft dependency: #2248 (Iron Law) defines finding classification; if #2248 ships first, use its classification rules as the source for deductions\\n- Blocks: trend analysis feature in `forensic-review` (no separate backlog item yet)\\n- Enables: fail-on-threshold CI gate pattern (not yet a backlog item)\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 three insertion points in one agent file (`code-reviewer.md`): Step 7 calculation rule, Output Format markdown template, STATUS block. No new files, no schema changes, no new MCP tools. Algorithm fully specified. Risk: score values in AC must be verified via manual trace.\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- `code-review-shared/references/severity.md` does not exist yet (#2250 not yet implemented). This item must inline the severity tier table in `code-reviewer.md` as a temporary owner. Implementation should include a note: \\\"Move this table to `code-review-shared/references/severity.md` once #2250 is complete.\\\"\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: NOT_APPLICABLE (feature not yet implemented \u2014 this is a pre-implementation grooming pass)\\n\\n**Design intent**: Add Health Score (0\u2013100, severity-weighted) to `code-reviewer.md` Step 7, Output Format, and STATUS block alongside the existing ternary verdict.\\n\\n**Current implementation state**: `grep -c 'Health Score' plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` = 0. The Health Score feature does not exist in any form.\\n\\n**Assessment**: NOT_APPLICABLE \u2014 the item describes a new capability, not a correction to an existing implementation. There is no existing implementation to assess alignment against. The current code-reviewer.md is internally consistent with its own design; the item correctly identifies that Health Score is absent and proposes to add it.\\n\\n**No divergence found**: The forensic-review/SKILL.md Step 3 verdict routing (PASS\u2192COMPLETE, NEEDS-WORK/FAIL\u2192NEEDS_WORK) is as described in the item. The complete-implementation skill reads the codebase-analysis artifact by verdict field \u2014 the Health Score addition is backward-compatible and will not break existing consumers.\\n\\n**Implementation note**: The item's Desired Structure (6 points) is consistent with the Output Format section structure in code-reviewer.md. The insertion points are correctly identified. No alignment gap between item spec and codebase design.\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\nSDLC layer blast radius \u2014 3 insertion points in one agent file, two read-only consumers.\\n\\nFiles changed: plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md \u2014 Step 7: add Health Score calculation rule; Output Format Summary: add Health Score field; STATUS block: add Health Score NN/100 line.\\n\\nFiles read-only affected: plugins/development-harness/skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md (additive, ternary routing unchanged); plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md (additive, new Health Score section is backward-compatible).\\n\\nDocumentation affected: None. CI/Configuration affected: None. Agents affected: None (reviewer-* use separate APPROVE/REJECT schema).\\n\\nSystems inventory:\\n- agents/code-reviewer.md (Producer) \u2014 computes verdict + Health Score\\n- skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md (Consumer) \u2014 reads verdict, routes to COMPLETE/NEEDS_WORK\\n- skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md (Consumer) \u2014 reads codebase-analysis artifact verdict\\n- research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md (Source) \u2014 lines 136\u2013148 define algorithm\\n- #2250 code-review-shared (Future owner) \u2014 will own severity tier table\\n- #2248 Iron Law (Soft dependency) \u2014 defines Critical/Warning/Suggestion classification\\n\\nEcosystem completeness: All 3 producers identified. All consumers verified backward-compatible. No stale docs. No agent instructions affected. Additive change \u2014 no deprecation required.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n**Rationale**: The review system lacks a quantitative signal that should exist. The ternary verdict (PASS/NEEDS-WORK/FAIL) allows two NEEDS-WORK reviews to be indistinguishable even when one has 1 blocking finding and another has 12. This means the orchestrator cannot detect whether remediation is improving or regressing \u2014 a decision gate that should exist but does not.\\n**Analysis Method**: none (missing-guardrail classification requires no root-cause analysis)\\n**Scenario Target**: `@dh:forensic-review` remediation cycles \u2192 orchestrator can compare Health Score across cycles to detect improving vs regressing implementation\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n**Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS**\\n\\n| Input | Status | Source |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Functional intent | AVAILABLE | Add Health Score alongside ternary verdict; algorithm fully specified (base 100, Critical \u221215, Warning \u22125, Suggestion \u22121, floor 0) |\\n| Scope boundaries | AVAILABLE | `agents/code-reviewer.md` Step 7 + Output Format + STATUS output; trend-tracking via `.brooks-lint-history.json` is out of scope for this item |\\n| Severity tier definition | DERIVABLE | Algorithm stated in item; authoritative shared file does not yet exist \u2014 `code-review-shared` (#2250) will own it; implementation inlines algorithm from item spec |\\n| Existing interface contracts | AVAILABLE | `code-reviewer.md` Output Format (lines 182\u2013233) and STATUS block (lines 237\u2013253) confirmed via Read: exact insertion points identified; `forensic-review/SKILL.md` confirmed present |\\n| Verification expectations | AVAILABLE | Measurable signal: 2 Critical + 1 Warning \u2192 `Health Score: 65/100`; `grep 'Health Score'` confirms field presence |\\n\\n**Gap**: Severity tier table is specified in this item but not yet canonically defined in `code-review-shared` (#2250). Implementation must inline the algorithm from the item spec. Once #2250 is delivered, the inline definition should be replaced with a reference to `code-review-shared/references/severity.md`.\\n\\n**Current state confirmed**: `grep -c 'Health Score' plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` returns 0 \u2014 the field does not yet exist. No prior implementation to update.\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\nN/A for feature items \u2014 this is a new capability request, not a defect or regression.\\n\\nThe change is verifiable via static check: `grep -c 'Health Score' plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` must return 3 or more after implementation. The measurable signal (2 Critical + 1 Warning \u2192 Health Score: 65/100) is verifiable by reading the Output Format section against the algorithm.\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nStep 7 of `agents/code-reviewer.md` produces both a verdict AND a Health Score:\\n\\n```text\\nVerdict: PASS | NEEDS-WORK | FAIL\\nHealth Score: 0\u2013100\\n  Calculation: 100 - sum(severity_weights)\\n    Critical: -15\\n    Warning: -5\\n    Suggestion: -1\\n    Floor: 0\\n  Severity per dimension defined in code-review-shared/references/severity.md\\n```\\n\\nThe STATUS output includes `Health Score: NN/100`. The artifact registered as `codebase-analysis` includes a `## Health Score` section with the score and the per-finding deduction breakdown. The forensic-review skill can compare scores across NEEDS-WORK remediation cycles to verify trend (improving vs regressing).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` Output Format Summary section contains a `Health Score:` field. STATUS block contains a `Health Score:` line. Run the agent on a sample with 2 critical and 1 warning finding \u2014 the artifact reports `Health Score: 65/100` (100 \u2212 15 \u2212 15 \u2212 5).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` Step 7 (\\\"Compute Verdict\\\") produces a ternary verdict \u2014 `PASS`, `NEEDS-WORK`, or `FAIL`. There is no quantified score, no severity weighting, no historical comparison.\\n\\nTwo reviews that both return `NEEDS-WORK` are indistinguishable, even if one has 1 blocking finding and the other has 12. The orchestrator (`plugins/development-harness/skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md`) cannot reason about whether code is improving or degrading across iterations of the same task.\\n\\nbrooks-lint's Health Score (common.md lines 14\u201315, AGENTS.md lines 15\u201316) provides the missing quantitative signal: base 100, deductions per finding (Critical \u221215, Warning \u22125, Suggestion \u22121), floor 0. Score is reported with each review and tracked over time via `.brooks-lint-history.json`. Allows fail-on-threshold checks (`fail-below: 70`) and trend analysis.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"328163053bac927801a6864d603088cc8719908aa37d20aeb17448cd2ba0d196\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPg2yXw\"}"}